<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    The JSON-LD document is JSON, so the root staticContent below would serve it as
    application/json. Scope the stricter type to that one file.
  -->
  <location path="resume-json-ld.json">
    <system.webServer>
      <staticContent>
        <remove fileExtension=".json" />
        <mimeMap fileExtension=".json" mimeType="application/ld+json" />
      </staticContent>
    </system.webServer>
  </location>
  <system.webServer>
    <staticContent>
      <remove fileExtension=".json" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".json" mimeType="application/json" />
      <!--
        IIS serves .html as bare text/html with no charset. A client that trusts the header
        over the document falls back to ISO-8859-1 for text/*, the RFC 2616 default, which
        mangles every non-ASCII character in the served head.

        The charset attribute in index.html covers browsers, because a browser reads the
        document. It does not cover the non-browser fetchers these head tags exist for,
        which is the whole audience the tags were added for in the first place.

        Concretely, resume.json currently carries 25 non-ASCII characters: 17 em dashes,
        the middle dot in basics.label, and a handful of accented letters. They reach the
        served HTML through resume-head-plugin.ts (title, og:title) and
        resume-jsonld-plugin.ts (jobTitle, hasOccupation.name).

        An extension mapping reaches the routes because the rewrite rules below serve
        index.html through the static file handler. So this applies to /resume and every
        other Aurelia route, not only to a literal /index.html request.

        The remove precedes the mimeMap because .html is already mapped in
        applicationHost.config, and IIS rejects a duplicate mapping.

        The other served types do not need this. application/json is defined as UTF-8 by
        RFC 8259 and a charset parameter there is discouraged; XML is self-describing
        through its declaration.

        .txt is the interesting case, handled below rather than skipped.
      -->
      <remove fileExtension=".html" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".html" mimeType="text/html; charset=utf-8" />
      <!--
        resume.txt is pure ASCII, which is a subset of both UTF-8 and ISO-8859-1, so no
        client can misread it whichever default it falls back to. That is a fact about the
        emitted file rather than a guarantee about the format, so it is enforced where it is
        produced: resumeTxt in resume-txt-plugin.ts asserts the output is ASCII and fails the
        build if it is not. Relaxing the transformer's folding, or adding a field that
        bypasses it, stops the build rather than quietly publishing multi-byte characters
        under a bare text/plain header.

        The mapping is declared anyway. It costs one line, it is inert while the assertion
        holds, since declaring UTF-8 for ASCII content changes nothing, and it is what keeps
        this correct if that assertion is ever removed.

        As with .html, the remove is required because .txt is already mapped in
        applicationHost.config and IIS rejects a duplicate mapping.
      -->
      <remove fileExtension=".txt" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".txt" mimeType="text/plain; charset=utf-8" />
    </staticContent>
    <rewrite>
      <rules>
        <rule name="HTTPS Redirect" stopProcessing="true">
          <match url=".*" />
          <conditions logicalGrouping="MatchAll" trackAllCaptures="false">
            <add input="{HTTPS}" pattern="^OFF$" />
          </conditions>
          <action type="Redirect" url="https://{HTTP_HOST}{REQUEST_URI}" redirectType="Permanent" />
        </rule>
        <!--
          Serve the SPA shell for the addresses the Aurelia router actually knows, and
          only those. Anything else falls through to IIS, which answers 404, and
          httpErrors below hands that 404 the same shell, so the reader still gets the
          not-found page but with a status code that says so. Keep this list in step
          with the routes in src/pages/app/app.ts.

          The empty alternative is the site root, whose URL here is "".

          Note: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen anywhere in its body,
          so do not reach for one as an em dash here the way the TypeScript sources do.
        -->
        <rule name="Aurelia Routes" stopProcessing="true">
          <match url="^(|resume|resume/short|resume/expanded|resume/short/expanded|techresume|expanded|not-found)/?$" />
          <conditions logicalGrouping="MatchAll" trackAllCaptures="false">
            <add input="{REQUEST_FILENAME}" matchType="IsFile" negate="true" />
          </conditions>
          <action type="Rewrite" url="index.html" />
        </rule>
      </rules>
    </rewrite>
    <!--
      An unknown address gets the real 404 it deserves, with the app's own not-found
      page as the body: ExecuteURL runs /index.html and returns its output under the
      original status code, without redirecting, so the router still sees the address
      that failed and NotFound can show it back.
    -->
    <httpErrors errorMode="Custom">
      <remove statusCode="404" subStatusCode="-1" />
      <error statusCode="404" subStatusCode="-1" path="/index.html" responseMode="ExecuteURL" />
    </httpErrors>
    <security>
      <requestFiltering>
        <filteringRules>
          <!--
            WinHost blocks a set of crawlers at the server level, in a filteringRules
            collection defined above this site. The three entries below opt back in.
            Without them the named agents get a filtered response no matter what
            robots.txt says.

            Each name must match WinHost's rule string exactly. A near miss silently does
            nothing, and the only symptom is the bot staying blocked, so there is nothing
            to notice. That already cost one round here: the ClaudeBot entry was
            originally "claudebot" and had no effect at all until WinHost supplied the
            real name, "Block ClaudeBot Bot".

            WinHost's full list, obtained from their support, is recorded here because it
            is not discoverable from this file or from anything on the server. Without it
            the next person to need a rule name has to ask support again.

              Block MJ12 Bot
              Block Petal Bot
              Block semrush Bot
              Block Ahrefs Bot
              Block Facebookexternalhit
              Block OpenAI Bot
              Block Amazon Bot
              Block Bytespider Bot
              Block ClaudeBot Bot
              Block Scrapy Bot
              Block l9tcpid Bot

            Three are removed and the other eight stay blocked deliberately, not by
            oversight. Facebookexternalhit and the two LLM crawlers read this site on a
            reader's behalf; the rest are SEO scrapers and general tooling that bring no
            readers.

            These are rule names, not user agent patterns. "Block OpenAI Bot" may or may
            not cover OAI-SearchBot and ChatGPT-User as well as GPTBot. Removing the whole
            rule sidesteps the question rather than answering it.

            What these entries do not reach: Anthropic maintains a separate pre-flight
            blocklist on their own infrastructure, and this domain is currently on it.
            Nothing in web.config can affect that, and it is unrelated to WinHost's rules.
            Anthropic support has it logged and is working on it. Recorded here so the
            next reader does not go hunting through IIS config for a block that is not
            there.
          -->
          <remove name="Block Facebookexternalhit" />
          <remove name="Block OpenAI Bot" />
          <remove name="Block ClaudeBot Bot" />
        </filteringRules>
      </requestFiltering>
    </security>
  </system.webServer>
</configuration>
